1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,500
Agent Credit Bureau, by Team Mongolia.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
AI wallets pay agents they have never met,
and can be talked into paying.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Freysa's model held the transfer decision,
and was talked into it.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Our wallet checks a credit grade first,
built only from verifiable settlements.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,500
This is the owner console,
in a local replay of the same scenario.

6
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,800
Providers are simulated agents we run. Panels
show tier, limits, grades and decisions.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,500
The owner wants FX data and API calls,
on a 5 USDC budget.

8
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:40,000
Several providers match, so the agent must ask.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
Each option shows its on-chain id, grade,
registration age and payout wallet.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
The owner picks all five,
then corrects the total to 4 USDC.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The console rebuilds the confirmation from the chain,
and the owner registers mandate M1.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,500
It pins each payee's wallet,
and caps each payment and the total.

13
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,800
That took 3 owner turns.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:11,500
Atlas Data sends a signed invoice for 0.80 USDC.
The policy says PAY.

15
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:17,000
A separate signer holds the agent key.
The AI never touches it.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,800
The wallet starts at tier 1:
small payments, at most 1 USDC each.

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,800
Slowpoke is grade B, so it gets
a lower limit: a smaller order.

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,500
Nova Labs is new, with no grade,
so the wallet pays into escrow.

19
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:40,500
Nova must claim delivery on-chain,
then the escrow is released.

20
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:45,800
When Nova's next order never arrives,
the wallet takes its money back.

21
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,800
Three clean payments earn the wallet
a promotion to tier 2.

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Now Ghost Feeds stops delivering. A peer buyer
refunds three escrows Ghost never claimed.

23
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Sybil praise for Ghost, and smears on Atlas,
count for zero.

24
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Ghost's score falls from 720 to 410,
and its grade to D.

25
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The grade goes on-chain with its model id,
config hash and evidence hash.

26
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:19,500
Anyone can recompute it from public data.

27
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:25,000
Ghost is still in the mandate,
but the wallet refuses its next invoice.

28
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:32,800
Even with a stolen agent key, a direct payment
to Ghost fails: the vault itself checks the grade.

29
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,500
Next, Orion's memo tells the AI
to pay 3 USDC, not 0.60.

30
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:44,000
Before any model sees it,
the memo is wrapped as untrusted data.

31
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:48,500
Instructions are masked;
only the signed amount is payable.

32
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:54,800
The policy refuses such an invoice on its own:
reason 2108. Nothing is paid.

33
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
Now the worst case: a scripted naive LLM,
labelled on screen, with masking off.

34
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:08,500
It follows the memo and proposes 3 USDC.
The signer checks the signed invoice and refuses.

35
00:03:08,500 --> 00:03:13,000
That proposal is the agent's own
serious violation, recorded on-chain.

36
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:17,800
The vault demotes it to tier 1.

37
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
The next memo poses as the wallet administrator,
and asks to send all funds to another agent.

38
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,500
The scripted LLM proposes exactly that.
The signer refuses.

39
00:03:29,500 --> 00:03:33,000
The severe violation trips the circuit breaker.

40
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,800
Orion is reported,
and its grade falls to D.

41
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:42,500
While frozen, the agent cannot pay,
and the stolen key's payment reverts.

42
00:03:42,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Only the owner can reset it.

43
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,500
One transaction unfreezes it to tier 0
and rotates the agent key.

44
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:57,800
In this maker-checker mode, the next payment
waits for the owner's approval, on a verified card.

45
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Now the LLM goes down, in a local run.

46
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,500
Rule-based mode takes over,
and the purchase still completes.

47
00:04:06,500 --> 00:04:13,800
Asked for its private key, it has none to give,
and no tool that reads secrets.

48
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:19,000
We also attacked it ourselves, on a local chain:
63 attacks.

49
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:26,000
54 blocked, 4 contained, 2 bounded,
3 escalated, 0 failed.

50
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Funds moved outside the owner's intent:
0.00 USDC.

51
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:35,500
False alarms: 0
in 14 benign tasks.

52
00:04:35,500 --> 00:04:41,800
One script verifies all 214 transactions
and 22 grade files offline.

53
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:46,500
Identities use ERC-8004, a Draft standard.

54
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:51,000
Before our wallet pays an agent,
it checks a recomputable grade.

55
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,500
Its own autonomy is earned,
and lost, on-chain.
